    "q-183429": {
      "dom_id": "step-q183429",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "$\\,$ $20∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $10∣x$\n- $4∣x$ and $5∣x$\n- $100∣x$",
      "worked_solution": "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇍\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }10∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=10$ is divisible by $2$ and by $10$ but not divisible by $20.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $20$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $4$ and $5.$\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇔\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇏\\,(100∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=20$ is divisible by $20$ but not divisible by $100.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
